<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- Flutter declares this in src/debug and src/profile only, so a release
         build ships without it and every provider call fails on the device
         with no error the app can distinguish from a dead network. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>
    <!-- There is deliberately no android:networkSecurityConfig here, and no
         android:usesCleartextTraffic. That is a decision (T-0361), not an
         omission, and this is what it permits and what it costs.

         WHY IT LOOKS NEEDED. Local recognition on this platform points the
         phone at an Ollama server the user names on their own network.
         Ollama speaks plain HTTP, and from targetSdk 28 Android blocks
         cleartext by default, so a network security config permitting it
         looks like the missing piece.

         WHAT IT WOULD ACTUALLY GOVERN: nothing this app sends. Android
         enforces that policy in its own network stack. Every request here is
         made by package:http over a dart:io socket, which the Flutter engine
         owns, and Flutter says so itself in the breaking change note for the
         network policy it landed and then reverted in 2.2: "The following
         only applies to platform native sockets (sockets owned by the Android
         and iOS platforms). Flutter does not enforce any policy at socket
         level; you would be responsible for securing the connection. If the
         socket is owned by Dart/Flutter, no policy will be enforced." The
         request to restore that enforcement, flutter/flutter#106678, was
         closed as not planned. So cleartext to the LAN already works, and no
         flag in this file is what makes it work.

         WHAT ANDROID COULD EXPRESS, AND IT IS NOT WHAT WE WOULD WANT. The
         config keys cleartext by name: <base-config> covers every
         destination, <domain-config> covers the exact hostnames listed under
         it. There is no CIDR notation, no address range and no wildcard, and
         it is a resource compiled into the apk, so an address the user types
         at runtime cannot be named in it at all. "Permit cleartext to the
         local network only" is therefore not expressible. The one option
         that is, the whole app one, would relax the platform rule for every
         destination in order to bless a single LAN address.

         WHAT IS AND IS NOT GUARANTEED, PLAINLY. The photographs travel to
         that server unencrypted and unauthenticated: anything on the same
         network can read them in transit, and this app does not wrap them in
         anything. Declaring nothing here does NOT prevent that; nothing in
         this manifest can. What it does is leave the platform rule as tight
         as it is today for the part of the app the platform does govern,
         which is every plugin and anything future that uses the Java stack.
         So the guarantee kept is about the plugins, and the exposure on the
         wire is real and is stated to the user where the address is typed
         (lanPrivacyWarning in lib/provider_config.dart, and the Local
         section of the settings screen). It is not softened in either place.

         WHAT WOULD CHANGE THIS. If the engine ever enforces the policy for
         Dart owned sockets, local recognition on Android stops working with
         a cleartext error and this comment is where to start. A runtime
         address still could not be named, so the only reachable option would
         be the whole app one above, with the cost above. That is a decision
         to take deliberately at that point, not a flag to add by reflex now.

         UNVERIFIED: none of this was exercised on a device. It rests on the
         two published statements quoted above and on the merged manifest of
         the release build, which carries no cleartext attribute from any
         plugin. -->
    <application
        android:label="shelfscan"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
